[C1108] gift-and-neighbour:entry fallback:; gift-and-neighbour:story body:
Luther says that good deeds do not earn acceptance by God. At this desk, consider why he still calls people to serve their neighbours and practise bodily discipline.

[C1109] gift-and-neighbour:completion/ending:
For Luther, helping neighbours and practising self-discipline matter. They serve people and train conduct, but do not buy acceptance by God.

[C1110] gift-and-neighbour:skip bridge:
For Luther, caring for a neighbour serves a real need without earning God's acceptance. The later role of good deeds remains disputed.

[C1111] gift-and-neighbour:story context heading:
Wittenberg, 1520

[C1112] marburg-table:objective:
What is received in Communion?
Does receiving the gift guarantee its benefit?
What can these opponents agree on?

[C1113] marburg-table:entry fallback:; marburg-table:story body:
Compare texts from the years before Marburg with its signed articles. The writers agree on some beliefs while disagreeing about Christ's bodily presence in Communion.

[C1114] marburg-table:completion/ending:
The Marburg signers agree on some beliefs and still disagree about Christ's bodily presence in bread and wine.

[C1115] marburg-table:story context heading:
Marburg, 1529

[C1116] response-and-growth:objective:
Does accepting a gift mean earning it?
Where do Luther and Trent agree?
What do later good deeds change?

[C1117] response-and-growth:entry fallback:; response-and-growth:story body:
The council distinguishes God's first help, an adult's response and later growth in righteousness. Compare these with Luther's claims without saying that either side teaches people to buy God's acceptance.

[C1118] response-and-growth:completion/ending:
Trent says God's help comes first and an adult can accept or refuse it. Luther and Trent still disagree about inward renewal and the later role of good deeds.

[C1119] response-and-growth:story context heading:
Trent, 1547

[C1120] friend-and-saint:objective:
Can a friend pray for us?
May we ask a departed saint to pray?
What is wrong with honouring this picture?
Whose bone is this?

[C1121] friend-and-saint:entry fallback:; friend-and-saint:story body:
Calvin's writings from 1543–1559 raise objections about prayer, images and relics. Compare asking a living friend to pray, asking a departed saint and honouring a religious picture or relic. Calvin objects to particular practices and claims; the cases must be considered separately.

[C1122] friend-and-saint:completion/ending:
Calvin rejects asking departed saints for prayer and giving religious honour to images or relics. Whether the bone belongs to the named person is a separate question that still lacks evidence.

[C1123] prayer-panel-fragment:objective:
Does asking saints put Christ aside?
Who receives the honour given to a picture?
Would proving the bone's identity settle the dispute?

[C1124] prayer-panel-fragment:entry fallback:; prayer-panel-fragment:story body:
The next day, we open the later replies at a cleared desk. Trent's replies disagree with Calvin about saints, images and relics. A new record also helps investigate the bone fragment. Decide which identity question it helps and which religious disagreements it leaves unchanged.

[C1125] prayer-panel-fragment:completion/ending:
The new record tells us more about who kept the bone, but gaps remain. Even a confirmed identity would not settle Calvin and Trent's disagreement about honouring relics.

[C1126] prayer-panel-fragment:skip bridge:
The record improves our knowledge of the bone's history. It does not settle whose bone it is, obtain a bishop's recognition or resolve the disagreement about honouring relics.

[C1127] prayer-panel-fragment:source-update arrival fact:
The archivist has sent one newly found custody record for this invented fragment case.

[C1128] prayer-panel-fragment:story context heading:
Trent, 1563

[C1129] plain-divinity-choice-unfair:wonder body:
Eating and enjoying comfort can be good. Taking the meal a neighbour needs is wrong because it puts personal comfort above what the neighbour is owed. The fault is in the choice, not in food itself.

[C1130] plain-divinity-limited-answer:wonder body:
Aquinas argues that fire can work well as fire by heating and burning while harming something else. This challenges the claim that every harmful effect needs a wholly evil source. It still leaves the question of why a knowing creator would make such a world.

[C1131] plain-divinity-reason-boundary:wonder body:
Aquinas thinks natural reasoning can establish some things about the cause of the world, but not the Trinity. He can therefore explain the belief in one God as Father, Son and Spirit without claiming that ordinary observations prove it.

[C1132] plain-divinity-help-not-erasure:wonder body:
Augustine says a person still chooses when helped by God. Aquinas says an axe really cuts while a craftsperson works through it. The limited comparison shows that dependent action can be real; it does not reduce people to tools.

[C1133] plain-divinity-no-paid-shortcut:wonder body:
Luther describes repentance as a lifetime of turning away from wrong. A seller's promise that a coin guarantees release for a dead soul cannot replace that changed life or give the seller control over God's mercy. His criticism does not declare every church rite or payment fraudulent.

[C1134] plain-divinity-bounded-agreement:wonder body:
At Marburg, the signers affirm one God as Father, Son and Spirit and receiving Christ through faith. They still disagree about his bodily presence in bread and wine. A shared signature records real agreement without erasing its limit.

[C1135] plain-divinity-gift-common-ground:wonder body:
Luther and the Council of Trent both deny, in this household comparison, that feeding someone buys the initial gift of being made right with God. This limited agreement does not make their accounts of faith, inward renewal or later good deeds the same.

[C1136] plain-divinity-evidence-not-consensus:wonder body:
A new record may help establish whose bone the fragment is. Calvin would still reject giving it religious honour even if its identity were proved, while Trent allows honour to genuine relics. Better identity evidence does not settle that religious disagreement.